<Editorial
  title="Privacy — BURS"
  description="How BURS handles your wardrobe photos and data. Short version: we don't sell, we don't train public models on user images, and you can delete everything from inside the app."
  path="/privacy"
  eyebrow="Privacy"
  headline="What we do, and don't do, with your closet."
>
  <p>BURS is a premium app. We sell subscriptions. We do not sell user data, we do not sell wardrobe photos, and we do not train public models on user images. The product depends on you trusting us with what's in your closet, so the rules below are absolute.</p>

  <p>This page is the summary. The full policy — the one that names every processor and every retention rule — is in the app under Settings → Privacy &amp; data, and it is the version that governs.</p>

  <h2>What we collect</h2>
  <p><strong>Photos and metadata of your clothes.</strong> Stored privately in your account. Visible only to you.</p>
  <p><strong>Account information.</strong> Email, password (hashed), subscription status.</p>
  <p><strong>Use signals.</strong> We record our own product events — screens opened, outfits generated, saved or rated, onboarding steps completed — against your account, so we can see which parts of the app work. This is first-party analytics in our own database. It is not sold and it is not shared with any advertising network. It is tied to your account rather than anonymous, which is why we say so here rather than calling it aggregated.</p>
  <p><strong>Crash and error reports.</strong> Sent to Sentry, carrying your BURS account identifier and the IP address the report came from, so we can tell whether a crash hits one person or everyone. Sensitive headers and personal-data keys are stripped before an event leaves the device.</p>
  <p><strong>Location, if you allow it.</strong> Used to fetch local weather so outfits suit the day. A fresh reading is requested at roughly city level, and the Android build only holds the approximate-location permission. If you would rather not share an exact position, switch Precise Location off for BURS in iOS Settings, or deny location and type a city name instead.</p>
  <p><strong>Stylist memory.</strong> The AI stylist keeps a short set of durable notes it learns from your chats — your style, your sizes, your climate, things you love or avoid. It is included in your data export, and deleting your account erases it.</p>
  <p><strong>Ad attribution.</strong> If you found BURS through an ad on the App Store, Apple tells us which campaign brought you: campaign, ad group, keyword, ad. Our subscription provider, RevenueCat, resolves that with Apple and attaches it to your account, so we can see which campaigns bring people who stay. That link is to your account rather than aggregated, which is why we spell it out here. It carries nothing about you or your closet, it stays between you, Apple and us, and it is never used to target you.</p>

  <h2>What we don't do</h2>
  <p>We do not sell user data to advertisers, brokers, retailers, or AI companies. We do not train publicly-available models on your wardrobe photos. We do not allow third-party SDKs to read your wardrobe. We do not display ads. We do not track you across other companies' apps or websites, and we do not use an advertising identifier.</p>

  <h2>Who else processes your data</h2>
  <p>A small set of processors operate the service. They only see what is necessary for their function.</p>
  <p><strong>Supabase</strong> — managed Postgres and storage holding your account, wardrobe and outfit data. <strong>Google (Gemini API)</strong> — the AI that powers styling; see below. <strong>Anthropic (Claude)</strong> — a standby used only when Google's API errors or times out, and only for garment photo analysis and for classifying what a chat message is asking; never for outfit generation or your style profile. <strong>Sentry</strong> — crash and error reports. <strong>RevenueCat</strong> — subscription receipt validation, and the Apple Ads attribution above. <strong>Stripe</strong> — payment processing if you subscribe on the web. <strong>Resend</strong> — transactional email. <strong>Expo</strong> — over-the-air app updates and push delivery. <strong>Open-Meteo</strong> and <strong>OpenStreetMap Nominatim</strong> — weather and city lookup; they receive a coordinate or a place name, never an account identifier. <strong>Google Calendar</strong> — only if you connect it yourself; see below. <strong>Discord</strong> — if you redeem a promotional offer code, a short internal alert goes to our own team channel carrying the code, the product, your country and the first eight characters of your account identifier. It carries no name, email or wardrobe content.</p>

  <h2>AI processing</h2>
  <p>Styling runs on Google's Gemini API. We send garment titles, tags, category, colour and material metadata, the relevant weather or calendar context, recent chat turns when you are talking to the stylist, and — only when you explicitly trigger image analysis — the image itself. Your style profile goes too, because it is what makes a suggestion yours rather than generic. We do not send your email, your payment details, your IP address or your coordinates. Google states that data sent via the paid Gemini API is not used to train its general models.</p>
  <p>If Google's API errors or times out, two of those calls fall back to Anthropic's Claude rather than failing: the garment-analysis call, which carries that garment's photo and metadata, and the short step that classifies what a chat message is asking for, which carries that message. Nothing else falls back.</p>

  <h2>Deletion</h2>
  <p>Every piece and every photo can be deleted from inside the app at any time. Account deletion removes your profile, wardrobe, outfits and chat history from our systems within 30 days.</p>
  <p>A few records outlive that deletion, and we would rather name them than imply otherwise. <strong>Crash reports already sent to Sentry</strong> carry your account identifier and stay until Sentry's own retention expires them. <strong>One deletion record</strong> — a random identifier, the date, and counts of what the account held, with no photos, content, chat or contact details — is kept so we can see how many people leave. <strong>Your record at RevenueCat</strong>, holding validated receipts and subscription state, stays until RevenueCat's retention expires it. Two more depend on what you did: if you ever paid on the web, <strong>Stripe</strong> holds a customer record with your email address; and if you redeemed an offer code, the <strong>Discord</strong> alert above stays in our team channel. Email <a href="mailto:hello@burs.me">hello@burs.me</a> and we will delete any of them.</p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>If you live in the EU/EEA, UK or Switzerland you have the right to access, correct, port or delete your personal data, and to object to or restrict processing. You can do it from inside the app — Settings → Account to download a copy of your data or delete your account, Settings → Privacy &amp; data to reset the stylist memory — or by emailing us. We respond within 30 days.</p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>Privacy questions: <a href="mailto:hello@burs.me">hello@burs.me</a>.</p>

  <h2>Google User Data</h2>
  <p><strong>Data Accessed.</strong> BURS requests read-only access to Google Calendar (calendar.readonly scope) solely to read calendar events. We do not access contacts, email, files, Drive, or any other Google data.</p>
  <p><strong>Data Usage.</strong> Calendar event data (title, time, location) is used exclusively to generate context-aware outfit recommendations within the BURS app. Events are processed only during the recommendation request to inform styling suggestions.</p>
  <p><strong>Data Sharing.</strong> BURS does not sell, rent, or share Google user data with third parties. Event titles and times are transmitted to our AI processing provider (Google Gemini API) solely to generate the outfit recommendation and are not retained by that provider beyond the request lifecycle.</p>
  <p><strong>Data Storage &amp; Protection.</strong> Google user data is transmitted over HTTPS/TLS. We do not persistently store Google Calendar data in our database; event data is held only in memory during the recommendation request. Infrastructure is hosted on Supabase (EU region) with row-level security.</p>
  <p><strong>Data Retention &amp; Deletion.</strong> Because Google Calendar data is not persistently stored, there is nothing to retain or delete on the calendar-data side. You can revoke BURS's access at any time at <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/permissions">https://myaccount.google.com/permissions</a>. To delete your BURS account and all associated app data, go to Settings → Account → Delete Account in the app, or email <a href="mailto:hello@burs.me">hello@burs.me</a>.</p>
  <p><strong>AI/ML Training.</strong> BURS does not use Google Workspace data to train or improve generalized AI/ML models. Calendar data is used only for in-session, per-user outfit recommendations.</p>
</Editorial>
